p='tmp/pdfs/create_reviewer.py'
s=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()
s=s.replace("('What is the main purpose of DaGOAL?','DaGOAL organizes real-life goals as trackable daily quests and makes completion visible through progress indicators and game-based rewards. Our formal research purpose and intended users are [state the approved manuscript wording].')", "('What is the main purpose of DaGOAL?','DaGoal is a gamified daily activity planner intended to help users reduce excessive screen time by replacing passive digital consumption with physical, mental, and creative activities. It uses preference-informed quests, flexible choices, progress feedback, and rewards to encourage participation.')")
s=s.replace("('What problem are you trying to address?','Our project is motivated by [documented problem from the problem statement]. The application responds with [specific implemented functions]. We do not claim it solves the broader problem for every user; our study evaluates [measured scope].')", "('What problem are you trying to address?','The capstone focuses on excessive screen use and passive routines, particularly among students and young adults. DaGoal offers structured real-world activities as alternatives and optional screen-avoidance quests. Our evaluation measures initial user perceptions after a short trial, not a causal or long-term reduction in screen time.')")
s=s.replace("('Who are the target users?','The intended users are [exact population/age/group from manuscript]. The Android implementation is designed for devices running API 29 or later; that technical minimum is separate from the study population.')", "('Who are the target users?','The manuscript identifies students and young adults as the intended audience. In the evaluation, respondents were reported as ages 19-26. The Android implementation supports Android API 29 and later; that device requirement is separate from the research population.')")
s=s.replace("('How are quests tracked?','Generic/manual quests are completed through app interaction; increment/target quests store current progress; step quests use Android sensors when available and permission is granted; avoidance quests rely on usage-access monitoring and overlay permission for interruption UI.')", "('How are quests tracked?','Generic quests use manual confirmation; count/repetition goals update stored progress; supported walking goals use Android step sensors when permission and hardware are available; timed screen-avoidance quests are started by the user and rely on Usage Access plus overlay permission for interruption UI.')")
s=s.replace("('Does it block or uninstall selected apps?','No. It monitors foreground usage for selected apps and can show an interruption overlay during an active avoidance quest. It does not remove apps or act as an OS administrator.')", "('The manuscript says blocking. Does the current build hard-block selected apps?','The current code monitors recent foreground app usage during a user-started avoidance quest and presents an overlay with a go-home action or timed ignore/reset options. Describe this observed implementation accurately; do not promise guaranteed OS-level blocking.')")
s=s.replace("('How did you test the application?','We tested [procedures] on [device/emulator models and Android versions], covering [flows]. Our recorded outcomes are [actual results]. Distinguish functional testing from usability/acceptance testing and cite only documented findings.'),('How did you measure effectiveness?','We used [instrument/criteria] with [sample size/respondent description] and analyzed results using [method]. Findings were [actual results]. Source code alone does not establish effectiveness; our answer comes from the methodology and results chapters.')", "('How did you functionally test the application?','The manuscript reports 97 manual functional test cases covering startup/authentication, offline use, onboarding, quest generation and replacement, task verification, rewards, shop/wardrobe, profile/streaks, permissions, synchronization, step tracking, and avoidance behavior. All 97 were marked Passed under the documented test conditions. This is functional testing, not proof of long-term behavior change.'),('How did you evaluate user perceptions?','Respondents used the app for three days, then completed an online software-quality questionnaire using a four-point agreement scale across usability, enjoyment/engagement, screen-time management, performance, and overall experience. The manuscript reports n=57 in the abstract/table, but its implementation-results text says 56 participants; reconcile the correct number before defense.'),('What were the evaluation results?','The reported grand mean was 3.31 (SD=0.30), interpreted as Strongly Agree. Domain means: Usability 3.42 (SD .39, Strongly Agree); Enjoyment/Engagement 3.22 (.46, Agree); Screen Time Management 3.40 (.38, Strongly Agree); Performance 3.25 (.40, Agree); Overall Experience 3.26 (.41, Strongly Agree). These are respondent ratings, not objective usage-time measurements.'),('Can you conclude that DaGoal reduced screen time?','Not definitively from this evaluation. The study lasted three days and relied mainly on participant feedback. The screen-time domain mean (3.40) indicates respondents felt the app raised awareness or encouraged better habits; it is not a measured before-and-after reduction or evidence of sustained effect.'),('What sample-count issue should the team clarify?','The abstract and results table label the evaluation n=57, while the implementation-results paragraph says findings were based on 56 participants. Check the raw response export and final analyzed dataset, then correct the manuscript or explain exclusions and state the valid n consistently.')")
open(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)
